# Overview
ietf-hardware-state module for netconfd, Matter over Thread implementation.

Reports Matter sensor devices, e.g. two IKEA ALPSTUGA air quality sensors, as
/hardware components (RFC 8348 ietf-hardware-state). Each Matter node is one
ianahw:container component, its measurements are ianahw:sensor child components:

 * <product>-<node-id>              - ianahw:container, mfg-name, model-name, serial-num,
                                       hardware-rev and software-rev from Matter Basic Information
 * <product>-<node-id>-temperature  - celsius, value-scale milli
 * <product>-<node-id>-humidity     - percent-RH, value-scale milli
 * <product>-<node-id>-co2          - other, ppm multiplied by 1000, value-scale milli
 * <product>-<node-id>-pm25         - other, ug/m3 multiplied by 1000, value-scale milli
 * <product>-<node-id>-air-quality  - other, Matter AirQualityEnum (1=good .. 6=extremely poor)

Any other Matter measurement cluster the device has (PM1, PM10, TVOC, CO, NO2, ozone,
formaldehyde, radon, battery) is reported the same way. If a node is offline its
sensors are reported with <oper-status>unavailable</oper-status>.

The netconfd module itself (ietf-hardware-state.c) only runs the helper
ietf-hardware-state-get (a symlink to the Python script ietf-hardware-state-matter)
when /hardware is retrieved. The helper reads the nodes from the Open Home
Foundation Matter Server (python-matter-server, the Matter controller used by
Home Assistant / Nabu Casa) over its WebSocket API. The Matter Server talks to
the Thread devices through an OpenThread Border Router.

 IKEA ALPSTUGA  x2
    |
    | Matter over Thread
    v
 Home Assistant Connect ZBT-2 USB dongle (Thread RCP)
    |
    v
 Raspberry Pi (or any Linux host)
  +- OpenThread Border Router (OTBR), REST API on :8081
  +- Matter Server, WebSocket API on :5580
  +- ietf-hardware-state-matter  <--popen--  netconfd (ietf-hardware-state module)

The helper uses only the Python standard library (own minimal WebSocket client),
so it runs unchanged on any host with python3.

# Dependencies
Installed netconfd and yangcli
 apt-get install netconfd yangcli libyuma-dev

python3 (standard library only)

OTBR and Matter Server, see example/docker-compose.yml:
 cd example
 ZBT2_DEVICE=/dev/ttyUSB0 INFRA_IF=eth0 docker compose up -d

# Installation
 autoreconf -i -f
 ./configure CFLAGS="-g -O0"  CXXFLAGS="-g -O0" --prefix=/usr
 make
 make install

# Forming the Thread network (once)
 docker compose exec otbr ot-ctl dataset init new
 docker compose exec otbr ot-ctl dataset commit active
 docker compose exec otbr ot-ctl ifconfig up
 docker compose exec otbr ot-ctl thread start

# Commissioning the sensors (once)
Use the Matter pairing code of each ALPSTUGA (the MT:... QR payload or the
11/21 digit manual code printed on the device/manual). New Thread devices are
commissioned over Bluetooth LE, the helper hands the Thread network credentials
(fetched from the OTBR REST API) to the Matter Server first:

 ietf-hardware-state-matter commission MT:Y.K9042C00KA0648G00 MT:Y.K9042C00KA0648G01

 ietf-hardware-state-matter nodes
 Matter Server ws://localhost:5580/ws: fabric 1, sdk 2026.1.0, thread credentials set: True, bluetooth: True
 node 1    online     IKEA of Sweden ALPSTUGA  serial=... component=alpstuga-1 clusters=0x005B,0x0402,0x0405,0x040D,0x042A
 node 2    online     IKEA of Sweden ALPSTUGA  serial=... component=alpstuga-2 clusters=0x005B,0x0402,0x0405,0x040D,0x042A

The devices stay commissioned in the Matter Server storage, after that the
module picks them up automatically (every node with a measurement cluster).

# Configuration (environment of netconfd / the helper)
 MATTER_SERVER_URL        ws://localhost:5580/ws   Matter Server WebSocket URL
 MATTER_NODE_ID           (unset)                  report only this node, see below
 MATTER_TIMEOUT           5                        seconds
 OTBR_REST_URL            http://localhost:8081    used by 'commission' for the Thread dataset
 THREAD_DATASET           (unset)                  hex dataset, overrides OTBR_REST_URL
 IETF_HARDWARE_STATE_GET  ietf-hardware-state-get  helper command run by the module

# Testing installation

 netconfd --module=ietf-hardware-state --no-startup --superuser=$USER

Other terminal:
 yangcli --server=localhost --user=$USER

yangcli pi@localhost> xget /hardware
...
